      "index": 0,
      "text": "B\n\nLet's analyze each option to see which one follows a similar logical structure:\n\nA) The students in class 3 are diligent and studious. Xiao Li is a student in class 3, so Xiao Li is diligent and studious.\n- This option is an example of affirming the consequent. It assumes that if a student is in class 3 (the whole), then the student must be diligent and studious (the part). This is similar to the dealer's logic, but it's not as strong because it doesn't explicitly mention a specific characteristic (like Yunshan tea) being produced in a certain place.\n\nB) The flying brand car is made in a certain country, and the car just now is not a flying brand, so it must not be made in the certain country.\n- This option is an example of denying the antecedent. It assumes that if a car is made in a certain country (the antecedent), then it must be a flying brand car (the consequent). The dealer's logic is different because it doesn't deny the antecedent but rather affirms the consequent.\n\nC) All drivers must have a driver's license. Xiao Zheng has a driver's license, so Xiao Zheng is the driver.\n- This option is an example of affirming the consequent. It assumes that if a person has a driver's license (the part), then the person must be a driver (the whole). This is similar to the dealer's logic, but it's not as strong because it doesn't explicitly mention a specific characteristic (like Yunshan tea) being produced in a certain place.\n\nD) A good doctor needs superb medical skills and noble medical ethics. Xiao Chen has both, so he is a good doctor.\n- This option is an example of affirming the antecedent and consequent. It assumes that if a doctor has both superb medical skills and noble medical ethics (the whole), then the doctor must be a good doctor (the part). This is different from the dealer's logic because it doesn't affirm the consequent but rather the whole.\n\nAfter analyzing all the options, the most similar to the dealer's logic is option A, which follows the same form of affirming the consequent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: A.\n</think>\n<answer>A</answer>",
