ive to customers while minimizing the risk of insufficient income to cover claims. The strategy is effective across different types of health conditions, as it focuses on customers who are less likely to submit claims for many years, thereby reducing the overall risk and ensuring that the income from the policies is sufficient to cover claims.\n\nThis comprehensive example demonstrates the versatility and effectiveness of targeting middle-aged customers unlikely to submit claims for many years, regardless of the specific health condition they might develop. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really help me understand how option D works in practice. Can you provide one more example with a different type of health condition to further illustrate the strategy? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> Certainly! Let's provide one more example with a different type of health condition to further illustrate how option D would work in practice.\n\n### Example Scenario\n\n**Context**:\n- Insurance Company X is considering a new policy for elderly people with diseases.\n- Premiums must be low to attract customers.\n- There's a concern that the income from the policies won't be sufficient to cover the claims.\n\n**Strategy**:\n- Attracting middle-aged customers unlikely to submit claims for benefits for many years.\n\n**Example**:\n\n**Middle-aged Customers**:\n- **Alex**: 45 years old, diagnosed with mild osteoarthritis 10 years ago. He has not had any serious health issues since then.\n- **Sophia**: 40 years old, diagnosed with mild rheumatoid arthritis 5 years ago. She has not had any serious health issues since then.\n\n**Benefits**:\n- **Alex**: If he were to develop a serious health condition in the future, he would be eligible for the policy.\n- **Sophia**: If she were to develop a serious health condition in the future, she would be eligible for the policy.\n\n**Premiums**:\n- **Alex**: Premiums are relatively low because he is middle-aged and unlikely to subm