ort the idea that option C is the correct answer to weaken the city's conclusion. \uff5cAssistant\uff5c>I hope this additional specific studies and data points help solidify the reasoning behind why option C is the correct answer. If you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask! \uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and specific studies. It really helps to understand the reasoning behind the conclusion. Could you also provide some examples of community engagement programs that might lead to an increase in library card issuance but not necessarily an increase in the number of unique users? <think>\nCertainly! Community engagement programs are designed to encourage citizens to use libraries more frequently. These programs can lead to an increase in the number of library cards issued, but they might not necessarily result in an increase in the number of unique users. Here are some specific examples of community engagement programs that can illustrate this point:\n\n### Examples of Community Engagement Programs\n\n1. **Book Clubs and Reading Groups**:\n   - **Example**: A city might launch a book club program where citizens can sign up for a book club and receive a library card. While the number of library cards issued might increase, the number of unique users might remain the same or even decrease. This is because many citizens might be part of multiple book clubs, each with its own library card.\n   - **Reasoning**: The increase in library card issuance is due to the same citizens participating in multiple book clubs, rather than an increase in the number of unique users.\n\n2. **Reading Challenges and Competitions**:\n   - **Example**: A city might organize a reading challenge where citizens can sign up and receive a library card. While the number of library cards issued might increase, the number of unique users might remain the same or even decrease. This is because many citizens might participate in multiple readin