messages:  ['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nSmithtown University\' s fund-raisers succeeded in getting donations from 80 percent of the potential donors they contacted. This success rate, exceptionally high for university fund-raisers, does not indicate that they were doing a good job. On the contrary, since the people most likely to donate are those who have donated in the past, good fund-raisers constantly try less-likely prospects in an effort to expand the donor base. The high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true, provides more support for the argument?\n# Options:\nA) This year the average size of the donations to smithtown university from new donors when the university\'s fund-raisers had contacted was larger than the average size of donations from donors who had given to the university before.\nB) The majority of the donations that fund-raisers succeeded in getting for smithtown university this year were from donors who had never given to the university before.\nC) This year most of the donations that came to smithtown university from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university\'s fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors.\nD) Smithtown university\'s fund-raisers were successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before about as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people.I think the correct option is: C\n\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the context and the question step by step. The context provides information about Smithtown University\'s fund-raisers and their success rate in getting donations. The key points are:\n1. The fund-raisers succeeded in getting donations from 80 percent of the potential donors they contacted.\n2. This success rate is exceptionally high for university fund-raisers.\n3. The high success rate does not indicate that the fund-raisers were doing a good job.\n4. Good fund-raisers constantly try less-likely prospects to expand the donor base.\n5. The high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort.\n\nThe question asks which option provides more support for the argument that the high success rate indicates insufficient canvassing effort. We need to analyze each option to see which one best supports this argument.\n\nOption A: "This year the average size of the donations to Smithtown University from new donors when the university\'s fund-raisers had contacted was larger than the average size of donations from donors who had given to the university before."\n- This option talks about the average size of donations from new donors versus previous donors. It does not directly address the canvassing effort or the success rate. It only provides information about the size of donations, which is not relevant to the argument about insufficient canvassing effort.\n\nOption B: "The majority of the donations that fund-raisers succeeded in getting for Smithtown University this year were from donors who had never given to the university before."\n- This option directly supports the argument. If the majority of the donations came from new donors, it suggests that the fund-raisers were primarily contacting people who had not donated before. This implies that the fund-raisers were not trying to contact more likely donors (those who had previously donated), which aligns with the idea that the high success rate indicates insufficient canvassing effort.\n\nOption C: "This year most of the donations that came to Smithtown University from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university\'s fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors."\n- This option suggests that many previous donors did not receive any contact from the fund-raisers. However, it does not directly address the overall success rate or the canvassing effort. It only provides information about the behavior of previous donors, which is not directly relevant to the argument.\n\nOption D: "Smithtown university\'s fund-raisers were successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before about as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people."\n- This option compares the success rate of Smithtown University\'s fund-raisers with other universities. While it provides a comparison, it does not directly support the argument about insufficient canvassing effort. It only suggests that Smithtown University\'s fund-raisers were not particularly successful compared to other universities in contacting new donors.\nThe correct option is:'] 40%|####      | 241/600 [1:50:12<16:05,  2.69s/it]
