he number line.\n3. **Solutions:** The solutions to a linear equation in one variable are the values of the variable that satisfy the equation.\n\n### Example:\nLet's consider the linear equation:\n\\[ 3x - 7 = 0 \\]\n\n### Step-by-Step Explanation:\n1. **Identify the Variable and Constants:**\n   - The variable is \\( x \\).\n   - The constants are \\( 3 \\) and \\( -7 \\).\n\n2. **Solve for \\( x \\):**\n   - Add 7 to both sides of the equation to isolate the term with \\( x \\):\n     \\[ 3x - 7 + 7 = 0 + 7 \\]\n     \\[ 3x = 7 \\]\n   - Divide both sides by 3 to solve for \\( x \\):\n     \\[ \\frac{3x}{3} = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n     \\[ x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\n### Summary:\nThe solution to the equation \\( 3x - 7 = 0 \\) is \\( x = \\frac{7}{3} \\). This means that \\( x = \\frac{7}{3} \\) is the value that satisfies the equation.\n\n### Real-World Application:\n- **Example:** Suppose you have a budget of \\( \\$7 \\) and you spend \\( \\$3 \\) per item. How many items can you buy?\n  - Let \\( x \\) be the number of items.\n  - The equation is:\n    \\[ 3x = 7 \\]\n  - Solving for \\( x \\):\n    \\[ x = \\frac{7}{3} \\approx 2.33 \\]\n  - Since you can't buy a fraction of an item, you can buy 2 items, and you will have \\( 7 - 2 \\times 3 = 1 \\) dollar left.\n\n### Summary:\nIn this example, the number of items \\( x \\) that can be bought is a linear function of the budget. The relationship is directly proportional, meaning that if you increase the budget, the number of items you can buy increases linearly.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation. It really helps to understand linear equations in one variable. If I have any more questions, I'll reach out. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad the explanation helped you understand linear equations in one variable. If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to reach out. Whether you ne