 to compare and analyze, further emphasizing how the way information is received affects behavior.\n\nThese examples clearly demonstrate how the way information is received (reading vs. watching TV, conflicting reports, etc.) influences people's behavior, supporting the choice of option B. Therefore, the correct option is indeed B: The way of information reception affects people's behavior. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you understand the reasoning behind choosing option B. If you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you provide more examples from the context to support option B? I want to make sure I fully understand the reasoning.\n\n<think>\nCertainly! Let's provide more examples from the context to further support why option B is the correct choice:\n\n1. **Disliking the value principle of a magazine vs. TV programs:**\n   - The context states: \"If you despise the value principle of a magazine, you can still read it with interest. If this happens to the TV program, you can't watch it and change the channel quickly.\" This shows that the way information is presented (magazine vs. TV program) affects the viewer's behavior. Disliking the value principle of a magazine doesn't affect reading interest, but it does for TV programs, leading to changing channels quickly.\n\n2. **Watching TV programs that you disagree with:**\n   - The context mentions: \"If you watch TV programs that you disagree with, you can't watch it and change the channel quickly.\" This indicates that disagreeing with the content of a TV program can lead to a quick change in channel, showing how the way information is received influences behavior.\n\n3. **Comparing conflicting TV reports:**\n   - The context states: \"When asked to choose from two conflicting TV reports, the audience either depends on intuition or what they believe before watching.\" This shows that the way information is presented (conflicting reports) 