messages:  ['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIn our solar system only one of the nine planets -- Earth -- qualifies as fit to sustain life. Nonetheless, using this ratio, and considering the astonishingly large number of planetary systems in the universe, we must conclude that the number of planets fit to sustain some form of life is extremely large.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the argument is questionable because it presumes which one of the following without providing justification?\n# Options:\nA) Life similar to earth\'s could evolve under conditions very different from those on earth.\nB) If a planet is earthlike, then life will arise on it.\nC) Our solar system is similar to many other planetary systems in the universe.\nD) Most other planetary systems in the universe have nine planets.I think the correct option is: D\n\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the context and the question step by step:\n1. The context states that Earth is the only planet in our solar system that can sustain life.\n2. It then uses this information to argue that given the vast number of planetary systems in the universe, the number of planets that can sustain life must be extremely large.\n3. The question asks us to identify the assumption that the argument presumes without providing justification.\n4. We need to analyze each option carefully to determine which one is not supported by the given context.\n5. Option A: "Life similar to earth\'s could evolve under conditions very different from those on earth."\n   - This option is not directly addressed in the context. The context only mentions Earth\'s ability to sustain life, not the conditions under which life could evolve elsewhere.\n6. Option B: "If a planet is earthlike, then life will arise on it."\n   - This option is not supported by the context either. The context only states that Earth is the only planet that can sustain life, not that all earthlike planets will have life.\n7. Option C: "Our solar system is similar to many other planetary systems in the universe."\n   - This option is not directly addressed in the context. The context only mentions our solar system and does not provide information about other planetary systems.\n8. Option D: "Most other planetary systems in the universe have nine planets."\n   - This option is directly addressed in the context. The context mentions that the argument is based on the ratio of planets in our solar system to the total number of planetary systems in the universe, implying that most systems have nine planets.\n9. Therefore, the argument presumes that most other planetary systems in the universe have nine planets, which is not justified by the given information.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe correct answer is D.\n</answer>\nThe correct option is:']
