and G cannot be assigned to the same department.\n3. **Condition 3:** If X is assigned to the sales department, W is assigned to the production department.\n4. **Condition 4:** F must be assigned to the production department.\n\nGiven that X and F are in the production department, we can start by assigning F to the production department:\n\n- **F** is assigned to the production department.\n\nSince F is in the production department, W must be in the sales department (Condition 3):\n\n- **W** is assigned to the sales department.\n\nNow, we need to assign H and Y to the same department. Since H and Y must be in the same department and F is already in the production department, H and Y must be in the production department:\n\n- **H** and **Y** are assigned to the production department.\n\nNext, we need to assign the remaining employees (G, I, and X) to the sales department and the public relations department. Since W is already in the sales department, we need to assign G and I to the sales department:\n\n- **G** and **I** are assigned to the sales department.\n\nFinally, X must be assigned to the sales department (since W is already there):\n\n- **X** is assigned to the sales department.\n\nThis leaves the public relations department for the remaining employee, which is G:\n\n- **G** is assigned to the public relations department.\n\nSo, the final assignments are:\n\n- **Production Department:** F, H, Y\n- **Sales Department:** W, G, I, X\n- **Public Relations Department:** G\n\nThis assignment meets all the given conditions:\n\n1. H and Y are in the same department (production department).\n2. F and G are not in the same department.\n3. If X is in the sales department, W is in the production department (X is in the sales department, W is in the production department).\n4. F is in the production department.\n\nThis example shows how the employees can be assigned to meet all the conditions. The correct option is indeed A, as G can be assigned to the sales department. L