      "text": "D) To develop the productivity of socialism, it is not necessary to take the road of reform.\n\nLet's analyze why the other options are incorrect:\nA) This option suggests that to strengthen socialist material civilization, we must establish a firm consciousness of taking the road of reform. However, the context does not explicitly state this. It only mentions that without this consciousness, reform cannot be achieved.\nB) This option implies that those who advocate strengthening socialist material civilization cannot agree not to take the road of reform. The context does not provide information about the opinions of those who advocate strengthening socialist material civilization.\nC) This option suggests that once the firm consciousness of taking the road of reform is established, the construction of socialist material civilization can be strengthened. The context does not support this conclusion. It only states that without this consciousness, reform cannot be achieved.\nTherefore, the only option that can be logically deduced from the given context is D. To develop the productivity of socialism, it is not necessary to take the road of reform. This is because the context emphasizes the necessity of reforming production relations to develop productivity, but it does not require a firm consciousness of taking the road of reform for this development to occur. Thus, the correct answer is D.\n</answer>",
