ent that the Han River should flow to the northwest.\n   - Option B implies that if fame and wealth do not exist for a long time, the Han River should not flow to the northwest. This is not directly stated in the context and seems to introduce a conditional statement not present in the original text.\n   - Option C states that fame and wealth cannot always be there. This aligns with Li Bai's statement that fame and wealth exist for a long time, implying that they are not always present.\n   - Option D suggests that if the Han River can flow to the northwest, then fame and wealth will always be there. This is not supported by the context, as the statement about the Han River's direction is separate from the permanence of fame and wealth.\n5. Conclusion:\nThe option that most closely aligns with Li Bai's point of view, based on the given context, is C) Fame and wealth cannot always be there.\n</think>\n<answer>\nC) Fame and wealth cannot always be there.\n</answer>\nThe correct option is:"]
