remises.\n- **Inductive Reasoning**: Use when the conclusion is likely to be true based on the premises, but it is not guaranteed.\n\nThese examples illustrate the use of inductive reasoning in real-world scenarios where the conclusion is based on specific observations and is likely to be true, but it is not guaranteed. If you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask! <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Thank you for the additional examples of inductive reasoning in real-world scenarios. Here's a summary of the key points:\n\n### Example 1: Market Research\n- **Observations**:\n  1. In the past 3 months, every customer who used Product A reported satisfaction.\n  2. In the past 3 months, every customer who used Product B reported satisfaction.\n- **Generalization**:\n  Therefore, using Product A is likely to lead to customer satisfaction.\n- **Reasoning**:\n  - The conclusion is likely to be true based on the observations, but it is not guaranteed.\n\n### Example 2: Epidemiology\n- **Observations**:\n  1. In the past 10 years, every flu season in a particular city has been preceded by a cold wave.\n  2. In the past 10 years, every flu season in a particular city has been followed by a rise in hospital admissions for respiratory issues.\n- **Generalization**:\n  Therefore, a cold wave is likely to be followed by a flu outbreak.\n- **Reasoning**:\n  - The conclusion is likely to be true based on the observations, but it is not guaranteed.\n\nThese examples demonstrate that inductive reasoning is used when the conclusion is based on specific observations and is likely to be true, but it is not guaranteed. If you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of deductive reasoning in a real-world scenario?\nCertainly! Here's an example of deductive reasoning in a real-world scenario:\n\n### Example: Deductive Reasoning in Legal Argumentation\n\n**Premises**:\n1. All men are mortal.